---
import PageLayout from "../layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, FACTS, FOUNDER } from "../consts";

const H2 = "font-heading text-xl font-semibold text-pz-ink";
---

<PageLayout
  title="Why Parenza — the story behind it"
  description="Parenza started as a structured decision system built for one family's first child by a fintech engineer who works with AI daily and has been through SOC 2, ISO 27001 and DORA audits. Here's what it was, and what it became."
  path="/about"
  heading="Why Parenza"
  subhead="It started as a system built for one child, not as a product idea looking for a market."
>
  <section>
    <p>
      Before our first child was born, I did what I do at work: I wrote things
      down. Not a diary — a structure. What we'd decided and why. What the NHS
      guidance actually said versus what the internet said it said. Which
      questions were feeding questions, which were sleep questions, and which
      only looked like one and were really the other.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4">
      Then the baby arrived, and the structure earned its keep. Every night we
      logged feeds and sleep. Every hard call — the reflux question, the vitamin
      D dosage where UK and Russian guidance disagree, when to start the nursery
      waitlist — went into a decision log with the reasoning attached. Within
      weeks there were{" "}
      <strong class="font-semibold text-pz-ink"
        >{FACTS.routineEntries} logged entries</strong
      >{" "}and{" "}
      <strong class="font-semibold text-pz-ink">{FACTS.knowledgeDocs} documents</strong>
      {" "}of family context behind them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class={H2}>Who I am, and why it shows in the product</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">
      I'm {FOUNDER.name} — {FOUNDER.role}, and before that Revolut, Deutsche Bank
      and a fintech I took from zero to launch as CTO. Fifteen years of building
      systems where being approximately right is the same as being wrong, plus a
      master's in computer and information systems security. More at{" "}
      <a
        href={FOUNDER.site}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        class="text-pz-clay-strong underline underline-offset-2">batiaev.com</a
      >.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3">Three things from that work ended up inside Parenza.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">
      <strong class="font-semibold text-pz-ink">I work with AI all day.</strong>{" "}
      Not as a novelty — as the tool I reason with. Which is why Parenza was
      never going to be a walled app with a chatbot bolted on. If the assistant
      you already use can read your family's actual record, that's worth more
      than another icon on your phone. That's what the MCP connection is for.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4">
      <strong class="font-semibold text-pz-ink"
        >I've been through the audits from the inside.</strong
      >{" "}
      SOC 2, ISO 27001, DORA — in businesses where a regulator asks for evidence
      and "we intend to" is not an answer. That experience is why the{" "}
      <a href="/trust" class="text-pz-clay-strong underline underline-offset-2"
        >trust page</a
      >{" "}reads the way it does: control by control, with honest statuses, and
      no badge we haven't earned. Data governance for a child's health record
      deserves at least the seriousness a bank applies to a balance.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4">
      <strong class="font-semibold text-pz-ink">I validate before I act.</strong>{" "}
      In risk systems you learn quickly that a confident number from an unchecked
      source is the most dangerous thing on the screen. Parenting advice has
      exactly that problem, at scale, aimed at exhausted people. So the
      specialists here name what they're working from, flag where national
      guidance diverges, and say plainly when a question needs a clinician rather
      than an answer.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class={H2}>The part that actually worked</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">
      The logging wasn't the valuable bit. The valuable bit was pointing an AI
      assistant at all of it and asking a real question — and getting an answer
      that knew this child's history instead of an average child's. Then
      splitting that into {FACTS.specialists} specialists with distinct remits,
      each with its own sources and its own stated limits, and putting a Chief of
      Staff above them to route questions and reconcile disagreements.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3">
      That's not a metaphor for how Parenza works. It is how it works, because
      it's what has been running on our own family since {FACTS.since} — before
      any of it became a product.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class={H2}>Why it isn't a tracker</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">
      Trackers are built around the log. You put data in and get charts back, and
      the charts don't help at 3am, because at 3am you don't have a data question
      — you have a decision. Parenza is built around the decision. The log exists
      to make the decision better.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class={H2}>Who it's for</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">
      Parents who want the reasoning, not just the reassurance — people who are
      comfortable being told "the guidance says X, your history says Y, here's
      the trade-off" and making the call themselves. If what you want is a
      soothing voice telling you it's all fine, there are gentler products than
      this one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class={H2}>Where it is now</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">
      Pre-launch, with a small design-partner cohort. No billing, no public
      signup, and no claims about scale we haven't reached. If this is how you'd
      want to make these decisions, tell us about your family on the{" "}
      <a href="/#early-access" class="text-pz-clay-strong underline underline-offset-2"
        >early-access form</a
      >, or email{" "}
      <a
        href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}
        class="text-pz-clay-strong underline underline-offset-2">{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a
      >.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-pz-ink-mute">
      Employers are named as professional background only. Parenza is an
      independent project, not affiliated with or endorsed by any of them.
    </p>
  </section>
</PageLayout>
